<h2 id="aged">Why It's Aged So Well</h2>
<p>What made Donald's testing profile so predictive wasn't any single number — it was the combination. A defensive tackle who is fast, strong, and explosive all at once is rare; most prospects at the position trade one quality for another. Statistical analysis of that combine class ranked Donald's overall performance as the best of any player at any position, more than two standard deviations above expectation in the 40, bench press, and 3-cone drill alike.</p>
<p>That same first-step quickness — the quality that let a 285-pound man run a 4.68 — is exactly what allows an undersized interior lineman to keep winning reps into his mid-30s, when most defensive tackles have long since lost the athletic edge that made them special as rookies. Explosiveness out of a stance tends to erode more slowly than top-end speed, which is part of why Donald's bet on himself isn't as far-fetched as a two-and-a-half year layoff might suggest.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Athletic Comparison</strong>
  Donald's 285-pound frame with a sub-4.7 forty is still an outlier a decade later. For comparison, most starting NFL defensive tackles run in the high 4.9s to low 5.0s at a similar weight — Donald was roughly three-tenths of a second faster than the position average, which at the line of scrimmage is the difference between beating a blocker to a gap and getting handled.
</div>
